  "answer_text": "Despite his name, Flounder from *The Little Mermaid* is **not actually a flounder**. \n\nReal-life flounders are flat, brown bottom-feeders that have both of their eyes on one side of their head. Disney\u2019s Flounder, with his round body and vibrant colors, is actually based on tropical fish. \n\nThe exact type of fish Flounder is depends on which version of the movie you are watching:\n\n### 1. The 1989 Animated Film: A Stylized Tropical Fish / Angelfish\nIn the original animated film, Flounder's design is heavily stylized. He is a generic tropical reef fish, with many marine biologists and fans pointing out that his round shape and bright coloring most closely resemble a type of **angelfish**. \n* *Note:* Within the universe of *The Little Mermaid* animated television series, characters (especially Sebastian) frequently tease him by calling him a **guppy**, which became a running joke.\n\n### 2. The 2023 Live-Action Remake: A Sergeant Major\nFor the 2023 live-action movie, Disney opted for photorealistic designs and officially modeled Flounder after a **sergeant major fish** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). The sergeant major is a type of tropical damselfish known for its roundish profile, yellow upper body, and vertical dark blue or black stripes.",
